  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\nMontana will be in SW flow aloft today with upper low over the\nPacific NW and upper ridge to the east. A humid airmass will\ncombine with the moist SW flow aloft today to create an unstable\natmosphere which will allow for chances for showers and\nthunderstorms at times. A few storms could become severe with\nlarge hail and damaging winds. PWATs measuring near the daily max,\nand well above the 90th percentile, provide stronger storms ample\nmoisture for high rainfall rates. HREF guidance suggests 1hr QPF\ngreater than 0.25\", and a 3hr QPF maximum of greater than 4\".\nWhile meeting this is unlikely, storms will be able to produce\ntorrential rainfall. Any storm that stalls or moves slowly may\nlead to localized flooding. NAM forecast soundings suggest very\nhigh MUCAPE and strong deep layer shear after 3Z. Given the\nisolated updraft helicity tracks during the same window values\nbetween 150-300 m^2/s^2 supports an isolated supercell or two. \n\nThe upper low moves into Montana on Monday as the surface low \nmoves north into Saskatchewan. Rain is expected to continue most \nof the time in the north while the models differ on how much the \ndry slot will limit rain in the south. The upper low moves to \nSaskatchewan and Manitoba tomorrow night and becomes stacked with\nthe surface low. Gusty west winds will develop during the day on \ntomorrow and continue through Tuesday with gusts to 40 to 50 mph \npossible. Wrap around rain is likely Monday night and Tuesday over\nthe NW half of the forecast area while the SE will be dry most of\nthe time. \n\nRainfall totals 1 to 2 inches are likely in the NW between tonight\nand Tuesday while some areas in the SE could end up with less than\na tenth of an inch. \n\nThe system exits the region Tuesday night and Wednesday and\nThursday are expected to be dry. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nUpped day 1-2 PoPs to further collaboration. Did not touch winds\nor wind gusts, but given how often we surpass NBM winds during \nstrong lows and 60kt 700mb winds, that will need to be a \nconversation with neighbors soon.",
